Dominic Raab: The government cannot apologize for its decision to isolate Spain.
In response to the government’s sudden decision to reimpose quarantine measures on Spain, Dominic Raab offered an explanation.
The ministers announced on Saturday that travelers who fail to return from Spain and its islands before midnight will be required to self-isolate for 14 days.
Spain has been on the list of “travel corridors” that can safely travel as set out at Number 10 Downing Street, but recent increases in coronavirus cases have raised public concerns about a second wave of the pandemic.
Sunday, Foreign Secretary made an explanation for the sudden reimposition of travel restrictions measures, saying that it was a “real-time reaction” to the surge in reported infections in the region.
He said that he had assessed the situation the previous afternoon and “we made our decision quickly.”
He added, “We cannot apologize for this.”
"We must be able to take rapid and decisive action, especially in cases involving our own country or Spain and specific countries, we must act."
Otherwise, Britain would face another infection risk, triggering a second wave of the pandemic, and then have to implement another lockdown.
The Minister of Transport faces mandatory quarantine like vacationers heading to Spain.
Following two days of reporting daily new coronavirus cases exceeding 900 in Spain, the government made this decision.
The Foreign Minister acknowledged that many tourists had already missed their chance to cancel or change their travel plans.
He said, “I know that for those who are in Spain or who have been considering going to Spain, this decision is very unkind, but we must be able to take swift and decisive action to protect Britain”.
“In reducing the number of cases and combating the resurgence of the epidemic, we have made some notable progress.”
Shadow Health Minister Jonathan Ashworth condemned the government's handling as “chaotic.”
He said that Downing Street’s sudden decision left holidaymakers “both bewildered and disheartened.”
This Labour Party member told Sophie Ridge, "I understand why they made this decision, but their decision-making over the past 24 hours has been chaotic."
“A holiday leavers in Spain will be confused and frustrated at this moment, as will those planning to travel to islands such as Spain and Tenerife, who wonder whether their employers will accept their isolation for two weeks.”
"The government is just saying 'we hope employers will cooperate.'"
"They made this decision without any discernible pattern in the past 24 hours."
The CDC notes that Salmonella is a bacterial foodborne illness that causes 1.35 million infections annually, leads to hospitalization of 26,500 people, and results in 420 deaths.
Common symptoms following Salmonella infection include diarrhea, fever, and abdominal cramps, which may appear within six hours to six days after exposure to the bacteria.
Symptoms usually last for four to seven days, and most people recover without treatment, but some cases require hospitalization.
A couple in California’s dog park was attacked during lunchtime because they were not wearing masks (video) - RT TV's US News
First came face masks as humiliation, then fists and feet in beatings.
A couple in California were confronted with this situation when they had lunch with their dog at a dog park. A woman sprayed pepper spray at them because they were not wearing masks while eating.
Ash O'Brien and her husband Jarett Kelley said that they were eating lunch at the Dusty Rhodes Dog Park in San Diego with their three-month-old Beagle puppy on Thursday.
When two spouses took off their masks and prepared to eat, an “elderly woman who suddenly appeared out of nowhere” walked up to them and called them “idiots” because they were not wearing masks, although they had said that eating with a mask was impossible.
O’Brien orders that woman to leave the park and stop her harassment behavior at this point, when she sprays pepper spray at both of them.
The video taken by the bystander began with Kelley competing with the woman for the spray tank and he was sprayed with a face.
After the attackers left with their dogs, O'Brien can be heard crying in the background.
O'Brien uploaded the video to Facebook and told ABC 10 that he wanted this woman “to go to prison.”
California mandates masks statewide, even in fresh air places.
People can only take off their masks during designated times, such as when eating.
However, the state's new coronavirus pandemic prevention guidelines do not mention that bystanders can use non-lethal weapons to urge enforcement of this provision.
Even in this “mask shaming” era, it is a crime that will result in a $1,000 fine or up to three years in state prison.
The boy from the Edo town who sold eggs refused to give bribes and was overturned; he received an apartment and free education of 100 rupees in exchange.
It was reported that on Thursday, Paras Raykar's car, which was full of eggs, was overturned by municipal officials in Indore.
The story of a 13-year-old boy who failed to sell eggs and had his car overturned by municipal officials spread on social media, and two days later, the boy from Indore received support from all over the country to help them recover their losses and restart their business.
The boy's family also said that politicians including Nationalist Congress Party leader Rahul Gandhi and Delhi Chief Minister Arvind Kejriwal had reached out to them and offered help as well.
The boy's family also received an apartment as a gift and a promise to cover their children's educational expenses.
It was reported that on Thursday, Paras Raykar’s cart selling eggs was overturned by Indore municipal officials, as the city implemented a staggered opening from left to right measure amid the pandemic.
In a video, a boy says that officials warned him earlier in the day that if he did not pay a bribe of 100 rupees, they would flip his car. The video quickly spread on social media.
The boy said that after he refused to hand over 100 rupees, those officials overturned his little car and smashed all the eggs.
The boy still says in the video that due to the impact of the epidemic, daily sales of eggs have dropped, and losses from these eggs will be an added burden for him.
Since Thursday, the family of the boy has received support from all over the country, with many people providing cash or kind assistance.
The locals also extended a helping hand to this family.
Former Chief Minister Digvijaya Singh funded them and helped the children enroll in a college; People's Party MP Ramesh Mendola also assured that a flat would be provided to the boy's family under the Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana housing scheme.
The Indore Journalists ' Club also provided food rations and money to boys.
The office of Congress Party leader Rahul Gandhi and Delhi Chief Minister Arvind Kejriwal also extended help.
Paras' grandfather said, "We have received support from many people all over the country."
Mr. Ramesh Mendola of the People's Party guarantees that we will be provided with an apartment under the Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana housing scheme.
He provided a bicycle and a grant of 2,500 rupees.
Digvijaya Singh contributed 10,000 rupees and also promised to look after the education of the two children.
Rahul Gandhi's staff also called us to offer help.
A female staff member of Chief Minister Arvind Kejriwal in Delhi also called us and helped us.
Jyothiraditya Scindia also supported it.”
The Home Secretary Priti Patel has asked Twitter and Instagram to explain the inappropriate content posted by Wiley.
Because Twitter and Instagram failed to promptly remove inappropriate content published by Wiley, Priti Patel is demanding an explanation from both platforms.
The police are currently investigating a series of anti-Semitic statements posted by this Grime artist on social media.
The Minister of Home Affairs tweeted on Sunday: "Wiley's anti-Semitic content is deeply offensive."
"Twitter and Instagram did not promptly delete these contents; I have asked them to provide adequate explanations."
Social media companies must act faster to remove these shocking hate speech from their platforms.”
He criticized Twitter for "ignoring antisemitism" because Wiley's tweets remained visible twelve hours after they were posted.
Subsequently, the website deleted multiple tweets and banned the account of the 41-year-old singer for seven days.
Twitter previously stated that it had temporarily suspended Wiley’s account “due to violations of our hate speech policy,” while Facebook said that “hate speech is not allowed on Instagram.”
On Saturday, the NYPD said, "The NYPD is extremely concerned about all reports of anti-Semitism. We are currently evaluating the relevant materials."
Meanwhile, Wiley’s agency A-List has also severed all relationships with this star.
The ex-agent of the singer, John Woolf, who is Jewish, wrote on Twitter: “Given the anti-Semitic content Wiley posted today, we @A_ListMGMT have severed all ties with him. There is no place for anti-Semitism in this society.”
Earlier, he had indicated that he neither supported nor condoned Wiley’s comments but would engage with him privately to “educate” him.
However, an Instagram video appears to show Wiley severing ties with his management company.
Other lawmakers also criticized Twitter for failing to remove the tweets promptly.
Labour MP Jess Phillips said on Saturday: "I just saw what Wiley published. Why is @Twitter allowing such blatant antisemitism and hate content to remain? It involves dangerous views: Jews have things you don't have, they control everything, they think they're superior, and so on. Such content is dangerous and should be deleted."
Representative Neil Coyle added, “It seems his PR company reacted faster than @Twitter did. I reiterate that we need to ensure social media platforms cannot be used to spread hate through legislation (including the Online Harms Bill).”
Government anti-Semitic independent adviser John Mann called the tweets "precisely what is needed to reflect why legal changes are necessary to allow for accountability."
The campaign to combat anti-Semitism also called for police to investigate Wiley’s content and called for the closure of his social media accounts “to prevent another spewing of anti-Semitic venom.”
Wiley, whose real name is Richard Cowie, is known as the "Grime Music Father" and was awarded an MBE in 2018 for his contributions to music.
Kudlow says next coronavirus relief package would provide more stimulus checks and extend eviction moratorium
White House economic adviser Larry Kudlow on Sunday pledged that the federal government will extend the moratorium on evictions and issue another round of stimulus checks under the next coronavirus relief package.
The week before Kudlow made his pledge, the White House’s proposal to reduce payroll taxes had been rejected by Congress.
The residents of Massart Mallawi village did not wish to comment on this matter and merely expressed condolences to the families of the children involved.
National Women and Children’s Committee advisor Khalid Darwish said in October that approximately 93% of Egyptian children between the ages of 1 and 14 have suffered from violence.
He also noted that prosecution is one of the most important means to eliminate child abuse, and stressed the need for enhanced cooperation between the Committee and the public prosecutors in this regard.
A research conducted by the National Committee on Women and Children found that children can also be subjected to violence even in places where they should be relatively safe, such as their homes, schools, or communities, with perpetrators including those who are supposed to care for them—such as parents or teachers.
The AP criticizes White House press secretary Kayleigh McEnany’s “politicized” briefing, but reporters keep asking political questions – RT America's US News
AP criticizes White House Press Secretary Kayleigh McEnany for making the press conference more political than newsworthy, worrying the media, but apparently forgetting that it was the journalists who chose those questions.
The largest US news agency, the Associated Press, published an article on Saturday stating that White House press conferences might "break tradition" because McEnany turned the press conferences into "short and flashy promotional and attack media venues for campaigning for a presidential re-election," while there was "a relative lack of information about what the government had done."
The last review is understandable as very little new information was disclosed at the press conference.
What the Associated Press did not mention is that almost no one asked for any new information at the press conference.
Journalists often use White House press secretaries to draw attention to themselves, and Jim Acosta of CNN took this tactic to the extreme.
They transcend the common adversarial relationship between the media and government, repeatedly asking loaded questions to doubt President Donald Trump.
In some instances, they choose to become the news themselves, such as when CBS reporter Weijia Jiang made an unambiguous implication in response to Trump’s retort of “ask China”: “Sir, why did you address this to me specifically?”
The launch of July 6 is a good example of how it is no longer a source of effective information for the American population.
On that morning, Trump tweeted that NASCAR ratings had declined because of false reporting about so-called hate crimes against driver Bubba Wallace and because he decided to ban displaying the Confederate flag during events.
Just as expected, reporters one after another asked McEnany questions related to this tweet, trying to get her to publicly admit that Trump supports the Confederate flag.
McEnany did not allow the reporters to get away with it, as saying so would damage Trump’s interests, but she repeatedly responded that the president has no position on the confederate flag, and his tweets are about NASCAR fans being wrongly portrayed as racists.
The Associated Press reports that she sounded “insulted” during the press conference.
The Associated Press story echoes the column published earlier this month by American Broadcasting Corporation journalist Jonathan Karl in The Washington Post, who is also President of the White House Correspondents Association.
Karl says that denying reality and using the White House podium for purely political purposes is contrary to public trust.
McEnany took over as press secretary in April and hosted her first press conference on May 1st, and her quick-wittedness and combative attitude made some Trump supporters see her as a hero.
Before she was appointed, the position of press officer had been vacant for more than a year.
Like other aspects of Washington's political life, the relationship between Trump-era White House press secretary and the media underwent significant changes.
This change came from both sides, having already occurred before McEnany's arrival.
At the White House Correspondents’ Dinner in April 2018, comedian Michelle Wolf subjected former press secretary Sarah Huckabee Sanders to vicious questioning, even MSNBC host Mika Brzezinski called it "shocking."
At McEnany's first press conference, Associated Press White House reporter Jill Colvin asked her: "Hey, can you promise never to lie to us on that podium?"
Fox News political analyst Brit Hume, a seasoned journalist with more than 50 years of experience, served as ABC's chief White House correspondent from 1989 to 1996 and says that today’s reporters exhibit “aggressive behavior” at press conferences because they look down on Trump.
He said on this week’s Tucker Carlson Tonight on Fox News that this was because reporters focused on countering Trump rather than collecting policy news.
"This is not just a game of cat and mouse," Hume said in discussing the media's intended purpose.
Our goal is not just to bring down government officials but to obtain information.
California protesters burn courthouse
Oakland (California) – Oakland police said that late Saturday night, peaceful protests in California turned into violent incidents. Protesters set fire to a courthouse, smashed police stations, and attacked several officers.
Local authorities in Auckland tweeted that protesters damaged windows, sprayed graffiti, and fired fireworks and used laser pointers at police officers.
Some have called for peace on Twitter, as seen in tweets that read: “We request organizers to help us provide safe spaces and locations for the demonstrators.”
Protests began on Saturday night, with organizations such as "Mothers Wall" (analogous to an organization in Portland, Oregon) participating in the demonstrations. At the same time, protesters confronted police deployed to the federal courthouse for security duties.
Since the May 25 incident in Minneapolis, where George Floyd was killed, President Donald Trump has sent federal police to suppress protests every night.
Initially, protesters held peaceful demonstrations in Auckland, but they later turned into violent clashes in the evening.
Around 11:30 PM, police declared the demonstration an "illegal assembly" and demanded that the demonstrators disperse.
According to news reports, a fire broke out at the Alameda County Superior Court, but the fire was brought under control in a short time.
The police said that at one point, protesters smashed windows and made racially charged slurs against residents.
In a photo posted on Twitter by the relevant authorities, the glass in the police station building was shattered and the walls were covered with graffiti.
UN senior officials canceled their visit to Pakistan due to flight reasons.
Islamabad (AP) – The incoming president of the United Nations General Assembly announced on Sunday that his visit to Pakistan had been delayed “due to technical problems with the flights.”
Last month, Turkish diplomat Volkan Bozkir successfully became the President of the 75th session of the United Nations General Assembly, which was originally scheduled to arrive in Islamabad on Sunday.
However, he tweeted that plans to visit the Pakistani capital had been postponed.
Pakistan's Foreign Minister Shah Mahmood Qureshi tweeted in response to Bozkir, saying that he warmly welcomes you to visit Pakistan as soon as possible for a constructive and fruitful visit.
Just one day earlier, Qureshi announced his trip enthusiastically and expressed his intention to discuss a range of issues with Bozkir, including Kashmir. Kashmir is located between Pakistan and its neighboring country India, where both claim sovereignty over the entire region.
Since achieving independence from British rule in 1947, there have been three wars between Pakistan and India, both of which involved the disputed region.
The two sides have frequently engaged in firefights in Kashmir, resulting in casualties among military personnel and civilians.
Pakistan hopes that the UN will adopt a resolution allowing people living in the Himalayan region to decide whether to merge with India or Pakistan or become independent, thus pushing for a solution to the Kashmir issue.
Manchester United secured a place in the Champions League, and Paul Pogba hinted at staying with the team.
After the Red Devils were locked in the top four, Manchester United star Paul Pogba hinted that he would remain with the team this summer.
Snatching Leicester City means Manchester United will finish third, ahead of Chelsea under Frank Lampard.
Before 12 more games, Leicester City was still leading Manchester United by 14 points, but the Red Devils made a strong run to reach the quarter-finals.
Pogba has twice requested to leave the team in the past two years, which has led fans to predict that he will depart this summer.
However, the pandemic means that few teams will be spending heavily this summer, and there is no buyer who can afford both the transfer fee and the wages needed to lure Pogba away from Old Trafford.
Making such a statement has been helpful for Pogba in recent weeks, as both his performance and body language have improved.
After winning the match 2-0, the French player posted on Instagram suggesting that he would stay with the team.
While Pogba may simply be celebrating Juventus' return to Champions League qualification, many fans interpret it as a strong hint that he intends to remain at the club to compete with top European players.
Pogba's current contract has one year remaining, but the club can extend it for another year, meaning he would actually be under contract for two more years.
Manchester United is keen to extend Pogba’s contract and wants to clarify his situation before the next season.
For Pogba, he feels comfortable in the team, and Bruno Fernandes' arrival has given him an additional boost.
This Portuguese midfielder became the heart of Old Trafford, allowing Pogba to advance deeper into the midfield and improve his mood.
Aubameyang helps Arsenal send Watford to the Premier League relegation zone.
Some of Watford’s players knelt to the ground, while others turned their heads away and left the field without looking back.
The whistle of Watford's return to the Premier League for the fifth year has just been sounded, but no one can say that Watford did not fight at a time when it was necessary for Watford to pursue Arsenal with all his might.
In fact, this was Watford’s most spirited and incisive performance in five years, as they were close to reversing the game when trailing 3-0 before succumbing to Arsenal.
With 16 minutes left in the match, Danny Welbeck curled a shot past the post, and goalkeeper Emiliano Martínez saved it. Had he scored, Welbeck would have given Watford their third point, levelled with Arsenal, and secured their place in the Premier League.
But in the end, Watford failed to achieve this, and their caretaker manager Hayden Mullins stated that if their previous form had been as good as it was in this match, he would not have had to exert himself so much.
Throughout the season, Watford struggled, and owner Pozzo’s management of the team was also chaotic.
A failed campaign may not destroy an empire, but the Pozzo family cannot lay the foundations for Watford to secure its return to the Premier League.
Watford's frequent changes in management have become an inescapable fate.
On rough calculation, the next reshuffle will be the 12th in eight years, but the influx of fresh blood from a change of leadership is limited.
Captain Troy Deeney faces relegation with the team, although he could still participate in the UAE Super League.
Some players may choose to leave, such as attacking midfielder Ismaïla Sarr, who again stated that playing in League Two in September is unbelievable, but preserving their strength is currently the top priority.
Arsenal coach Mikel Arteta said that the unpredictability of the match strength between the two teams makes the game more exciting.
Arteta also said that Watford’s forwards have been “cheating,” before later switching to the footballing term “trying to stay up.”
Arsenal saying this is not without justification; Arsenal is ahead in the score, and Watford must press relentlessly to catch up.
Watford conceded a penalty kick right at the start.
In the first 40 seconds, Craig Dawson accidentally tripped Alexandre Lacazette, and after a lengthy verification of about three minutes via VAR, a penalty kick was awarded. Pierre-Emerick Aubameyang scored the penalty kick.
Watford press hard, Sarr quickly counter-attacks with the ball on the right side, with Roberto Pereyra and Troy Deeney providing cover as they close in on the goal.
A former Labour Party member told The Daily Mail that even if Starmer expelled Corbyn from the party, they would still bring legal action.
What I mean is that he really made the Labor Party competitive again.
He has a long way to go in terms of policy, but he has already made an impressive start, having succeeded in re-engaging many who had previously abandoned the Labour Party and no longer considered themselves Labour voters, and viewing him as a serious and earnest person."
Blair indeed fears that the Labour Party is still seen as a party that only raises taxes.
He said, "According to my experience, if you're a member of the Labor Party, then in tax matters, you have to be very careful because the public is not sitting there worrying whether or not you’re going to raise taxes; they are more likely worried that you will raise taxes, so you need to pay attention to this mindset."
Coronavirus Will Change the World Economy Permanently (Opinion)
Like various security measures became commonplace after the 9/11 events, virus screening may become part of our lives as well.
It is imperative to make necessary investments in infrastructure to prepare for future virus testing.
If coronavirus immunization is only a short-term effect, this investment can provide security for economic operations.
During the pandemic, many economies implemented policies similar to Germany’s “short-time work” (Kurzarbeit) subsidy.
Under this policy, working hours and wages will be reduced accordingly, with any shortfall in wages to be subsidized by the government.
This move maintains the link between businesses and workers, laying a solid foundation for rapid economic recovery.
Improving policy implementation is also important; these policies need to become permanent components of economic recovery tools.
Remote work is likely to become increasingly common.
There is evidence that working from home can be just as efficient as working in an office.
However, many companies remain reluctant to adopt remote-working arrangements.
Given that remote work has been widely tried and proved effective, this approach may continue.
With e-commerce further developing, remote healthcare, video conferencing, online teaching, and financial technology have seen increasing adoption, with the pandemic crisis accelerating digital transformation.
Companies involved in cross-border supply chains are struggling with various shortages and bottlenecks.
We may see many such companies shifting production back to their home countries.
Finance Minister Fiona Hyslop has said that the pandemic has had a "catastrophic impact" on all activities.
The restrictions put in place to prevent the spread of the coronavirus have affected Scottish festive activities and sporting events like Highland Games.
Ms. Hyslop said, “While we have taken an encouraging step, such as the resumption of drive-in theaters, many industries will still be unable to operate for some time.”
We have been engaging in close and constructive collaborations with industry organizers, who have come up with creative solutions for how to operate safely given their respective challenges.
In addition to direct funding, some of the funds will be used to promote innovation and sustain long-term recovery of the event activities.”
The Event Industry Advisory Group notes that the industry is among the first to be restricted by lockdowns and will likely also be one of the last to see comprehensive easing of restrictions.
The Chairman of the Group, Peter Duthie, who also holds the post of Executive Director of the Graggs Securities and Exchange Commission, said that the competition had generated nearly £6 billion annually for Scotland.
He said, “It is necessary for the government to allocate additional funds to the industry, which has received widespread support from event organizers and numerous related supply chain enterprises that have suffered severe economic losses during prolonged lockdowns.”
Dr. Shivraj Singh Chouhan is confirmed to have contracted COVID-19.
Chief Minister of Madhya Pradesh, Shivraj Singh Chouhan, confirmed Saturday that his coronavirus test results were positive and said in a tweet posted Sunday morning that he was “fine” and praised those on the front lines of the fight against the pandemic.
The chief minister reiterated his warnings from yesterday and called on everyone to abide by the guidelines for preventing the spread of disease, such as maintaining social distance and wearing masks.
"Friends, I am fine. The frontline fighters risk their lives selflessly to fight the epidemic; they all deserve commendation. I am honoring all the frontline fighters across the country," Chowhan tweeted from Chirayu Hospital in Bhopal, where he was admitted yesterday.
“Keeping a distance of two meters, washing hands frequently, and wearing masks – these are our most effective weapons against the coronavirus,” he said, “I hope everyone can use these weapons to protect themselves and their families.”
Zhouhan added finally, “Be careful everyone, I hope you all stay safe and healthy.” This is also what I pray to God for.
Saturday, Chouhan tweeted that he had tested positive for the virus despite his best efforts to avoid such an occurrence; he warned that even a little bit of carelessness could lead to infection with the coronavirus.
This morning, Chouhan tweeted that while the threat of infection still exists, there is "no need to be afraid," and called for those who have symptoms to get tested.
He says, "If you get infected with the virus, there's no need to be afraid. Do not hide your symptoms; inform the doctor immediately and begin treatment. Early treatment can lead to recovery."
Central Bing now has over 27,000 confirmed cases, with 7,600 active cases, and 799 deaths related to the coronavirus.
In recent weeks, India has seen an increase in confirmed cases, with daily new infections exceeding 45,000 since July 23rd.
The total number of confirmed cases of COVID-19 is rapidly approaching 1.4 million, with over 467,000 active cases and over 32,000 deaths linked to the virus.
And, for studying rocks on Mars, what could be more suitable than a rock from Mars itself? says Professor Caroline Smith, curator of the Meteorite Gallery at the Natural History Museum.
Smith is also a member of the “Mars 2020” science team and adds that scientists believe the rocks they returned were from Mars.
The tiny bubbles within the meteorite are identical to the composition of Mars' atmosphere, which is why we are certain that this rock came from there.
It is believed that this Martian meteorite was formed when a small asteroid or comet collided with Mars about 60 to 70 million years ago, scattering fragments into space.
One of the fragments swept through the solar system and eventually landed on Earth.
This meteorite, now known as SAU 008, was discovered in Oman in 1999 and has been kept by the Natural History Museum since then.
On the instruments aboard the "Perseverance" rover, there is a high-precision laser instrument named SherLOC, which will be used to decipher the chemical composition of Martian rocks and determine whether they contain organic matter that could indicate the presence or continued existence of life on Mars.
Adding a SAU 008 is to ensure maximum precision for this operation.
Smith added, “We selected this rock particularly because its chemical composition matches ours, and it is a very hard rock.”
One of the Martian meteorites we found is very fragile, but this one is as hard as an old boot.
Upon locating valuable rocks of research interest, "Perseverance" will store them on the Martian surface.
Then these rocks will be sent to Mars robots for recovery and then launched from space to Earth for research analysis.
A man was shot near Mambourin, nearby Verville.
Last night, a man in western Melbourne was shot and rushed to the hospital for emergency treatment.
The police arrived at Mambourin, near Vereby, around 8 PM after receiving an alarm. On Yard Street, they found a 20-year-old man with multiple gunshot wounds to his lower body.
The man was taken to the hospital for treatment and is reportedly in good condition with no life-threatening injuries.
The police said they were investigating this case.
Oliver Spencer's Sunday: "On Sundays, I don't dress up; I wear loungewear."
Question: How did your Sunday start?
I stumbled down the stairs on my way to make tea for my wife and was knocked over by a dog.
After disembarking from the ferry, we drove up to the A3 motorway and headed back to London to prepare for the start of our workday. Upon arriving home, the children went to bed while I came back after walking my dog and had another bowl of cereal.
I would be happy to return from White Island to London, because I know that if I did not, I would not have a home on White Island.
Kenyan police in Garissa arrested for shooting incident
Two Kenyan police officers have been arrested on suspicion of causing two deaths in a shooting incident.
The shooting occurred on Saturday at the Soko Ng’ombey market in eastern Garissa County, where protests were ongoing over an alleged murder case happening inside the market.
Two victims were Aden Abdi Madobe and Muhiyadin Adow Shibin.
The Kenya Police Inspectorate has launched an investigation into the shooting incident.
The investigation process includes forensic analysis of the involved firearms.
Aden Duale, a Garissa lawmaker, called the incident "heinous" and condemned "police brutality" in a post on Facebook.
Several activists also expressed concerns about police enforcement actions during the pandemic.
According to data from Amnesty International, since March, Kenyan police have caused 21 deaths, all due to failure to comply with curfew orders and wearing masks as health requirements.
At the beginning of this month, police in the capital city of Nairobi used tear gas and arrested multiple demonstrators who had participated in the annual Saba Saba march – which originated from protests 30 years ago against then-President Daniel arap Moi’s dictatorship.
A front-line reporter says that protests this year were more radical due to the public anger sparked by the police crackdowns during the pandemic lockdown.
Security upgraded outside the U.S. Consulate General in Chengdu, China
On Sunday, security at the U.S. Consulate General in Chengdu, China was upgraded, and U.S. personnel were preparing to evacuate.
Three moving trucks entered the building, with uniformed police and plainclothes police lined up along both sides of the street, and metal barriers set up along the roadside.
China's Global Times editor posted a tweet stating that the US Consulate General in Chengdu was required to close within 72 hours, i.e., at 10 am on Monday, July 27th (0200 GMT).
China criticizes some personnel of the US Consulate General in Chengdu for “engaging in activities inconsistent with their identity.”
China’s Foreign Ministry spokesperson Wang Wenbin did not offer any details and simply stated that such activities interfere with China’s internal affairs and undermine its security interests.
China demanded on Friday that the US consulate in Chengdu be closed as a countermeasure to the US’s demand for the closure of China’s consulate in Houston, Texas.
Washington demands that China close the Consulate General in Houston “in order to protect American intellectual property and the personal information of Americans.”
After the Chinese Consulate General in Houston closed on Friday, a group of individuals resembling American officials entered the facility through the back door.
Wang Wenbin said on Saturday that this behavior seriously violates international law and bilateral agreements, and China will respond, but did not specify the measures.
It further exacerbated tensions between the two countries over trade and industrial espionage as well as human rights issues by closing embassies in a confrontational manner.
The pranksters provide wrecking tools, and in the zoo, baboons wave all sorts of implements.
The primates of the Nosley Wildlife Park in Mercesside County are notoriously notorious — they will pounce on cars parked inside the fence, rain scratchers or rear-view mirrors.
Some saw baboons at the North Wales Wildlife Park in Merseyside wielding knives, screwdrivers, even chainsaws.
Staffers fear that pranksters will provide these annoying primates with weapons and they will pounce on stopped cars, smashing windshield wipers or rear-view mirrors.
Now, staff are worried that visitors may give them lethal weapons as “jokes,” which is increasing their aggression, and some have even seen the monkeys rifling through toolboxes.
A park staff member told the Sunday Times: “We are not sure whether the tourists who wanted to see them attack the cars provided them with weapons or whether they stole them from their pickups and trucks”.
"They do indeed rummage through others' toolboxes and carry them around. One has seen baboons dragging a chain saw."
Another staff member said, "We have seen baboons carrying knives and screwdrivers. I wonder if tourists handed them those."
Chimpanzees attacks were visible, even to the repairmen in northwest England who knew about this zoo.
A repairman from Manchester Salford said that people knew what was going on at the zoo, and two of his clients were bitten by monkeys this year.
They said that one minute the children wanted to see the monkeys getting into the cars, and the next minute, the license plates were gone, but such problems have brought good business to repair shops.
North Shore Zoo insists that it is as safe as a McDonald’s drive-thru, as long as visitors stay in their vehicles during the 5-mile wildlife park tour. The zoo reopened on June 15th, taking safety measures to address the pandemic.
This zoo covers about 222.7 hectares and houses animals such as lions, tigers, rhinos, zebras, and camels, dismissing reports of them having weapons for baboons.
A spokesperson said, “We believe that many such stories have been exaggerated through hearsay and embellished, leading to situations where items being found in the park seem unbelievable.”
In 2012, car manufacturer Hyundai placed its new model i30 car in the zoo with 40 monkeys for ten hours to prove the vehicle's strength is sufficient to ensure the safety of families with children.
Manchester United and Chelsea advance to the Champions League; Barnsley and Watford fall out of the Premier League.
On Sunday, Manchester United and Chelsea advanced to the UEFA Champions League by defeating Leicester City in the final day of the Premier League, while Brighton and Hove Albion and Watford were relegated, ending their five-year peak battle.
After Bruno Fernandes and Jesse Lingard scored, Manchester United defeated Leicester City 2-0, securing third place for Ole Gunnar Solskjaer’s team.
Chelsea defeated Wolves 2-0, thanks to goals and assists from Mason Mount, securing fourth place and overtaking Leicester City (which had been consistently in the top four during the season) to claim the final spot in the UEFA Champions League.
Leicester City will participate in the European league, and so will Tottenham, which drew 1-1 with Wolves in a match at Crystal Palace and ultimately advanced due to a better goal difference than Wolves.
To avoid the last-minute clash for the remaining two promotion spots, Aston Villa scored in the 84th minute and equalized one minute later with a goal from the substitute, leaving them tied at 1-1 with West Ham United.
Captain Jack Grealish scored a goal for Aston Villa, which may be his last match for his childhood club.
Bournemouth defeated Everton 3-1 but finished one point behind Aston Villa.
Watford were relegated as the second-from-last team after losing 3–2 to Arsenal.
Manchester United scored more than 100 goals during the league season and won 5-0 against relegated Norwich, securing a place in the Europa League. This match was also David Silva's final Premier League game as he played for Manchester United for 10 years.
Kevin De Bruyne breaks records with 20 assists and two goals this season.
Liverpool defeated Newcastle United 3-1, ending their title quest for this season, with the club finishing with a score of 99 points.
In addition, Southampton won 3-1 against Sheffield United and Danny Ings has scored 22 goals this season.
And Leicester City player Jamie Vardy has scored the most goals and won the Golden Boot.
Gwyneth Paltrow hopes to instill good "values" in her children.
Gwyneth Paltrow said that she tries to educate her children with “not just giving them fish,” hoping to help them develop good values.
Paltrow has two children with her ex-husband Chris Martin, aged 16 (Apple) and 14 (Moses), and she hopes they develop good values rather than relying on their parents' fame.
"Why, among other things, I believe it is important to cultivate a child’s ethical character and ensure that their values align with either your own values or their own sense of identity," she told Rob Lowe in the podcast.
“Reality is that I can point you in the right direction, but if you just try like everyone else, you won’t get anywhere.”
She adds, “As parents, what I can do for my children is teach them what is right from wrong and what work is. I indeed tried to avoid just handing them fish.”
Paltrow also took steps to keep children’s lives as private as possible, ensuring that their social media accounts remained private.
Given that there will be new elections, the Renewal Party eventually agreed to join the coalition government.
Ted Cruz says Pelosi wants "to solve problems with money and force the US government to shut down."
WASHINGTON – Texas Republican Senator Ted Cruz criticized House Speaker Nancy Pelosi and the Democratic Caucus for the coronavirus relief package they have been pushing, accusing it of aiming “to solve problems with money and shut down America,” and negotiations over subsequent measures continue.
Cruz said on Sunday during an interview with Face the Nation that the US is facing two simultaneous crises: a global pandemic that has taken more than 600,000 lives worldwide, and an "absolute economic disaster."
Cruz criticized Pelosi for “not addressing any of them” during an interview with “Forbes,” before he agreed to it.
Cruz said that her goal is to use money to resolve problems and prevent a government shutdown in the US.
"You especially need to look at the $3 trillion bill she is trying to push, which is nothing more than throwing money away rather than actually solving problems. Our goal should be to get Americans back to work and allow them to support their families."
The Senate Republican Party and the White House consulted last week on the terms of the fourth phase of the programme to deal with the new crown epidemic, which is expected to reach $1 trillion.
Last weekend, Chief of Staff Mark Meadows and Treasury Secretary Steven Mnuchin returned to Capitol Hill to further discuss with Senate staff. On Sunday, Mnuchin, in an interview with Fox News Channel, stated that Majority Leader Mitch McConnell of the Senate would unveil the details on Monday.
The most pressing issue facing lawmakers and the focus of the debate between Republicans and Democrats is that extended unemployment benefits have expired and will be canceled this weekend.
The Democrats want the “Phase IV” coronavirus relief package to continue including the additional $600 in benefits, while Republicans call for cutting this amount, arguing that some Americans receive unemployment income that exceeds their wages.
Cruz says that currently half or more Americans receiving increased unemployment benefits earn more than they did when working. He notes that some small business owners have encountered difficulties hiring due to this, as he learned from speaking with small business owners in Texas.
He says, “Of course they won’t come back to work, because the federal government is paying them sometimes twice as much as their wages.”
Cruz believes that Congress should focus on passing a recovery bill to ease taxes and regulations for small businesses so that Americans can return to work.
He said, “The Recovery Act is not about spending tens of trillions of dollars, but pausing payroll taxes, which amounts to giving a raise to every working American. This is the way that truly gets people back to work.”
President Trump last week required that the next bailout package include cuts to payroll taxes; but Mnuchin said that proposal would not be included in the legislative process because Republicans do not accept it.
President Trump confirms that the next bill does not include a payroll tax cut, but attempts to blame it on the Democrats.
Last week, Cruz objected to the cost of the new proposal, stating on Sunday that he disagreed with the measure proposed by Senate Republicans.
However, he also criticized the $3 trillion bill passed by the Democratic-controlled House in May, accusing his opponents of deliberately crafting measures that would harm the president’s interests before the November election.
He said that “the Democrats’ sole objective is to defeat Donald Trump; they naively believe that the best way to defeat him is to shut down every aspect of American life and close every school in America.”
Prime Minister Narendra Modi mourns the Kargil heroes and says Pakistan is trying to stab India in the back.
Today is the 21st anniversary of the Kargil War, and Indian Prime Minister Narendra Modi, while paying tribute to military martyrs, stated that despite India's efforts to maintain friendly relations, Pakistan continues to try to stab India in the back.
Prime Minister Modi said during his monthly radio program "Mann ki Baat" that "Pakistan caused this disaster; they devised cunning plans to grab Indian land and divert their domestic conflicts."
He said, “Pakistan is shameless for attempting to occupy Indian territory and diverting public attention from its internal turmoil.”
At that time, India was working to repair its relationship with Pakistan.
But, as it is said, the essence of evil is to fight with everyone for no reason.
Such people will only do evil, even if the other party wants to do them a good deed."
He said, “That is why Pakistan chose to ignore India’s efforts to maintain the relationship and stabbed it in the back.”
He added, “Imagine the enemy entrenched atop high hills, while our troops fight below them. But our high spirits and true strength helped us defeat the enemy on the mountains.”
"Our courageous armed forces demonstrated India's great power in the Kargil War," Prime Minister Modi said, recalling what he had seen and experienced during his visit to the war zone.
India celebrates an event annually to commemorate the Kargil victory, marking the day in 1999 when Indian Army recaptured all of India's outposts in the Ladakh region that were occupied by Pakistani forces almost 21 years ago.
On July 26, 1999, the Indian Army announced that "Operation Vijay" was concluded successfully.
The war that took place on the snowy high grounds of Ladakh region lasted for nearly three months and resulted in the death of over 500 Indian soldiers.
Earlier today, several high officials and military leaders also paid tribute to the martyrs at the “Unknown Warrior Memorial” (Amar Jawan Jyoti) at the National War Memorial, including Defence Minister Rajnath Singh, Deputy Defence Minister Shripad Naik, Chief of Staff Bipin Rawat, Chief of Army Staff M M Naravane, Chief of Air Staff R K S Bhadauria, and Admiral Karambir Singh of the Navy.
Relations between India and Pakistan have been tense for decades after the Kargil War, marked by a series of terrorist attacks.
India insists that negotiations and terrorist attacks cannot take place at the same time and Pakistan needs to destroy all terrorist camps operating within its territory.
Online lending firms issue a warning: Small and medium-sized enterprise loan schemes may not achieve their goals.
Small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs) typically do not receive much favor from large banks, so they increasingly turn to peer-to-peer lending platforms to meet their funding needs.
However, Mr. Callaghan noted that peer-to-peer lenders do not have an advantage over large banks, as they cannot access the same cheap funding, and he also warned that the small and medium-sized enterprise guarantee scheme is unpopular.
He said that I think this scheme is unlikely to achieve its purpose; people call us expecting cheap funds from us rather than half of the loan amount being provided by the government.
So, after we called them, they were very disappointed because someone had expressed a desire for a discounted price, but our financing costs remained unchanged.
Mr. Callaghan added that such a scheme would be “a piece of cake” if fintech enterprises were able to obtain cheaper financing and then transfer the funds to small businesses.
This is somewhat of a dilemma – large banks can obtain funds at lower costs, but they prefer to avoid risks, so they do not lend in the same way. They will not access data unless there is collateral, in which case they feel secure. Otherwise, they would be uncomfortable, ” he said. “Unless we can get cheaper funding, I don’t think the market has any illusions about the popularity of this solution.”
Moula in Melbourne also participated in the formulation of the plan, CEO Aris Allegos stated that the 50% guarantee yield suggested in the government scheme should be transferred to small businesses, but different lending institutions would handle it differently.
He said, "I can understand that others may not have experienced such a cost structure."
“This may relate to their financial structure or to the types of loans they make to whom they make loans and how they assess the risks of these categories.”
Mr. Allegos declined to disclose how much lending was under the Moula scheme but said that the revised plan “concentrates on reflecting the changes that need to be made,” thereby providing greater flexibility for both borrowers and lenders.
But he is very concerned about the “two-speed economy” that is running and its impact on small businesses.
He said that for small businesses, the biggest problems faced by Moula and loan institutions are cliffs and how to manage them.
Overall, while there has been some growth in demand, it has not yet returned to pre-pandemic levels.
The online lending institution Prospa, listed on the Australian Securities Exchange, also refrains from disclosing the amount of loans it has disbursed under the scheme, citing this as sensitive commercial information. However, CEO Greg Moshal noted that the government’s expansion of the scheme has been well received.
He said, “We support various enhancements to the solution that enable small businesses to access a range of financing products tailored to their needs, including flexible short-term cash flow products.”
Virginia Beach city demolishes Southern Alliance monument
Virginia Beach (VJ) - On Sunday, Virginia Beach City removed a 115-year-old South Alliance monument from the public area. Two days ago, the City Council unanimously decided to dismantle it.
According to media reports, workers dismantled the 27-foot (8.2-meter) tall monument on Saturday morning.
The monument was erected in front of the former Princess Anne County Courthouse in 1905, where slave auctions also took place.
On Thursday evening, the city council unanimously decided to proceed with the related demolition work.
The city council heard from dozens of speakers who viewed the monument as a source of division for many and constantly bringing to mind painful pasts.
Given recent protests and vandalism against other monuments, the monument in question has been completely covered and surrounded by locked gates.
The monument will be temporarily retained.
The city now needs to wait for a response from historical organizations or other relevant entities willing to handle this monument.
Protests continue; police try to use new low-lethal tools
In Portland and across the country, violent clashes continue between law enforcement and protesters, with debates raging about how to reduce police use of lethal force.
Some companies are ramping up efforts to provide new, high-tech tools like police-controlled handcuffs, which are intended to replace more dangerous weapons such as pepper spray, rubber bullets, and flashbangs.
Amnesty International analyzed 500 videos from protests that took place from May 26 to June 5, and found 125 instances of excessive use of force in 40 states and the District of Columbia.
Brian Castner, an Arms Investigator with International Crisis Group, said, “We saw the misuse of tear gas, pepper spray, pepper balls, and 40mm impact rounds, all used inappropriately in combination with various non-lethal weapons.”
Some still hold the view that choice is important for avoiding gun use.
Corey Pegues, a former deputy inspector of the New York Police Department, stated, “We should provide police officers with various personal protective equipment instead of lethal weapons like guns, as policing is the only profession in America where citizens grant the power to take a life.”
There is a new tool called the Bola Wrap, a handheld device that can launch a 8-foot Kevlar rope to ensnare suspects.
Other new types of equipment include long-range acoustic devices (also known as sonic cannons), directed energy weapons that heat up human skin with laser beams, and new kinds of artillery shells made from materials such as foams and chalk dust, as well as stink bombs and electric stun guns.
Just days after George Floyd was killed by police, protests turned violent, and the stock price of gun manufacturer Axon surged over 18%, hitting an all-time high.
The market for lethal weapons with low lethality is expected to reach $11.85 billion in 2023.
Watch the video to learn about the new high-tech non-lethal tools used by police, how to regulate their use, and what risks these tools may pose to future policing and communities.
UN says that thousands of Pakistani insurgents are stationed in Afghanistan
Islamabad – A UN report states that over 6,000 Pakistani insurgents are hiding in Afghanistan, with the majority being illegal members of the Pakistani Taliban organization responsible for attacking military and civilian targets in Pakistan.
According to a report released this week, the organization known as Tehreek-e-Taliban (TTP) has links to the Islamic State’s (ISIS) branch in Afghanistan.
Some members of TTP even joined an organization affiliated with IS located in eastern Afghanistan.
On Sunday, the Afghan government did not respond to an Associated Press request for comment on related issues.
Reports indicate that ISIS-K (Islamic State in Khorasan Province) in Afghanistan has been severely hit by Afghan security forces and US and NATO forces, sometimes also facing attacks from the Taliban in Afghanistan.
The report was written by the Analytical Support and Sanctions Monitoring Group, which is responsible for tracking terrorist organizations around the world.
The report estimates that there are around 2,200 members of the IS group in Afghanistan, despite its leadership being weakened, which still considers a Syrian citizen, Abu Said Mohammad al-Khorasani, one of its leaders.
The report also notes that monitoring teams reported that two senior ISIS commanders, Abu Qutaibah and Abu Hajar al-Iraqi, had arrived in Afghanistan from the Middle East.
The report notes that although Islamic State (IS)-held territory is shrinking, they still have the capability to carry out attacks of some scale in various regions of the country, including Kabul.
The organization also wants to bring together the Taliban fighters who opposed the agreement with the United States.” The agreement here refers to the peace agreement signed by the United States with the Taliban in February.
The aim of this agreement is to allow the United States to end its 19-year-long war in Afghanistan and to hope that the Taliban will guarantee that their territory will not be used by terrorist organizations.
It is expected that the agreement will also ensure that the Taliban fully commit to fighting IS.
The second part of the agreement – and possibly the most important part – calls for talks between Taliban representatives and political leaders in Kabul.
Later on Saturday, the U.S. State Department issued a statement saying that its peace envoy Zalmay Khalilzad was still busy in the region, trying to push forward negotiations that had been repeatedly postponed due to disputes over the two sides' plans to release prisoners.
This US-backed agreement calls for the Afghan government to release 5,000 Taliban prisoners, in exchange for which the Taliban would release 1,000 government and military personnel as a gesture of good faith prior to negotiations.
Currently, the Afghan government refuses to release about 600 of the Taliban prisoners, calling them high-profile criminals, but proposes to release only the remaining prisoners.
The Taliban rejected the government's proposal.
This is the closest opportunity in history for both sides to engage in internal negotiations and the next key step in ending the 40-year war in Afghanistan, the State Department said.
"While important progress has been made in exchange of prisoners, further efforts are needed to solve the problem."
Earlier this week, the Taliban’s political spokesman said that the Taliban plan to hold talks with Kabul’s political leaders after Eid al-Adha (an Islamic holiday) next month, provided that the prisoner release work is completed first.
The biggest concern for Pakistan is the militants within Afghanistan, particularly those linked to the TTP, Jamaat-ul-Ahrar, and Lashkar-e-Islam, or the Balochistan Liberation Army, which launched large-scale attacks in southern Sindh and southwestern Balochistan that month.
Multiple Pakistani soldiers were killed in combat with militants in southwestern Balochistan province.
The TP organization carried out an extremely deadly attack on Pakistan in 2014 that resulted in the deaths of 140 members of the Pakistani Army Academy.
Most of them are students, some as young as five years old.
The report states that “foreign terrorist groups operating in Afghanistan pose a threat to the security of two countries, with an estimated number of between 6,000 and 6,500 people, most of whom are members of the TTP.”
HSBC, Britain's largest bank, denies setting up a trap for Chinese company Huawei in the US investigation – RT Business News
HSBC, a London-based multinational bank, said via social media that the bank had not fabricated evidence against Huawei as reported by Chinese media, leading to the arrest of Huawei's chief financial officer in Canada.
A now-unavailable WeChat post stated that banks claimed their actions did not trigger an investigation by the US into Huawei, the world’s largest supplier of telecom network equipment, as scrutiny into Huawei’s alleged violation of US sanctions against Iran on Iran had already begun well before the banks got involved, in late 2016.
Bank stated on Saturday that "HSBC has no hostile intent towards Huawei and there is no situation of 'frame-up' Huawei," and added that it would never manipulate any facts about its customers.
To respond to the information request from the U.S. Department of Justice (US DOJ), HSBC Group merely stated objective facts.
HSBC neither ‘fabricated’ evidence nor ‘covered up’ the facts.”
The platform has blocked this article and is warning it as “in violation of regulations.” The article content cannot be seen now.
After multiple Chinese media outlets (including those with government ties) accused HSBC of colluding with Washington to set up a trap for Huawei, HSBC quickly issued this explanation.
In December 2018, Canadian authorities arrested Huawei's CFO Meng Wanzhou at the request of US agencies, and for nearly a year and a half, the company has been a focal point in diplomatic news.
Ms. Meng faces fraud charges for allegedly using shell companies to violate sanctions against Iran and concealing Huawei’s relationship with Iranian company Skycom from banks.
The new evidence cited by The Global Times shows that prior to this, HSBC was very aware of the relationship between the Chinese company and Skycom.
It is reported that the bank chose to “collude” with US authorities in order to avoid harsher penalties imposed in 2012, when HSBC had already been forced to admit to money laundering and violations of sanctions, including those related to Iran.
At that time, the bank was ordered to pay around $1.9 billion in fines and needed to implement measures to prevent similar misconduct.
In an article on the matter, the newspaper wrote: “There is evidence that HSBC was aware of this relationship but deliberately sold its clients’ confidential business documents to the US side in order to let the US government overlook the British bank’s improper behavior and avoid the penalties it should have faced under the Deferred Prosecution Agreement of 2012.”
This Chinese newspaper, under government control, adds in an open warning that if such collusion with banks is confirmed, it may impose "severe punishment" on them, in China (one of HSBC's main markets).
In recent weeks, this well-known bank has been the target of repeated criticism.
Last month, HSBC was criticized for supporting the implementation of the new national security law in Hong Kong, which is the source of HSBC's business and contributes more than half of its profits.
HSBC finds itself in an awkward position: US Secretary of State Mike Pompeo calls it “bowing and scraping” in the West; while in the East, Chinese media hail its “late but supportive” stance.
Angola: Pedalé Hospital transferred to Ministry of Health supervision
Ruoan da – Republic President João Lourenço transferred on Friday the oversight of the Pedro Maria Tonha Pedale hospital under construction in Luanda to the Ministry of Health.
The Office of the President of the Republic stated in a declaration that this move is aimed at implementing the government's construction principles, expanding the hospital network, and ensuring better medical and pharmaceutical services for the public.
It also notes that “in the current exceptional circumstances, this measure allows the Angolan people to have a differentiated and valuable hospital with modern technology and precision instruments for diagnosing and treating complex diseases.”
The article notes that President of Angola’s decision also takes into account the country's population growth, which has fueled a “huge demand” for the most diverse range of high-quality healthcare services.
The document also notes that this demand is reasonable, as hospitals encompass complex disease treatments, teaching, and research related to ophthalmology and maternal and child health, areas for which strategies and management should fall under the purview of the Ministry of Health.
Algeria: President Tebboune mourns Mahmoud Ghamama – Algeria has lost a patriot and a steadfast warrior.
Alger – President of the Republic Abdelmadjid Tebboune expressed his condolences for Mujahed Mahmoud Guemama, a deceased member of the National People’s Assembly who passed away on Friday, describing Algeria as “a nation that has lost a patriot and a warrior dedicated to the cause of the people and the country.”
President Tebboune wrote in the condolence letter: "Today, Mujahed Mahmoud Guemama has left us. Algeria has lost a patriot, a fighter who dedicated himself to the nation's cause alongside the people, who did not waver from the moment he took up arms. His resolve was unwavering, and he fought side by side with valiant mujahideen brothers until he took his last breath on this day of profound sorrow, and we could only listen to the will of God."
"Late Mahmoud Guemama, may he rest in peace, was born into an exceptional generation of patriots who were steadfast believers and sacrificed for their country."
"In 1960, they joined the Tahart training camp of Wilaya VI at the height of their lives, joined the ranks of the revolution and liberated Algeria from the claws of inferior colonialism, sacrificing their lives."
This deceased leader possessed a fighting spirit and dedicated himself to building an Algeria that was united and prosperous; his name is now household knowledge.
The President added: “In the far south, Mahmoud Guemama is a revered mediator. Once any sparks of discord are ignited, he steps in to help his compatriots reach reconciliation.”
These qualities and values won Mahmoud Guemama the respect and trust of the people of Tamanrasset, electing him as a representative in the National People's Assembly for multiple terms, a messenger and defender of the region's concerns and aspirations of the people, and the voice of all Algerian people.
"There is no tribute that can praise Mujahed Mahmoud Guemama; may he rest in peace."
"Today, he left us at the most critical moment when Algeria needed him," said the President.
Finally, President Tebboune said, “I pray to the Almighty Allah that the deceased may be bathed in the light of your holy mercy, in the endless heaven, and greet his arrival alongside the shuhada and the pious.”
I pray that Allah gives his family and loved ones patience and comfort… Only you have such supreme power.”
Travel Guide for Coronavirus Pandemic: Countries Implementing Entry Restrictions for Bulgarian Passengers
More countries are implementing travel restrictions. Starting this week, the Czech Republic hopes to conduct coronavirus tests on seasonal workers from Bulgaria. Denmark has imposed that only Bulgarian citizens who have registered their residence, hold employment contracts, or possess business travel documentation can enter the country.
Starting today, people who have visited Bulgaria and Romania in the past 14 days must undergo mandatory quarantine in Italy. Finland only accepts travelers with 'justifiable reasons' for entry.
Austria requires mandatory quarantine at home even if PCR test results are negative. Germany also implements the same policy for arrivals. For the next 14 days, entry is not permitted to southern Romania without sufficient reason. Greece has taken stricter measures on its land borders.
The Ministry of Foreign Affairs announced that as of Tuesday, persons arriving in Greece from Bulgaria must present a negative PCR test result to Greek border authorities, with the test needing to be completed within 72 hours prior to entering Greece's southern neighboring country.
They also announced that travelers must present corresponding test certificates to be allowed entry. The certificates must be issued in English and include the three names and number found on the traveler's passport or ID. If the certificates do not meet these requirements, authorities in neighboring southern countries will not accept them.
Passengers must undergo nasopharyngeal swab testing at the extraction port/lab to complete an RT-PCR test in a laboratory. The accepted results include those obtained from tests conducted on samples taken from national reference laboratories, national health laboratories, or private labs that have been certified by relevant national accreditation bodies (not necessarily dedicated to testing for the novel coronavirus).
Greek citizens, residents in Greece, and travelers with significant professional reasons are exempt from this measure.
The Greek Ministry of Foreign Affairs also announced that passengers arriving in Greece on Tuesday, July 28, and Wednesday, July 29, who did not undergo testing before departure will be tested upon arrival by Greek authorities.
The following requirements remain in effect: passengers traveling to Greece by air must fill out and generate a QR code for the electronic Passenger Locator Form (PLF) at least 24 hours in advance, and submit the form to Greek authorities upon arrival in Greece. For more information, see travel.gov.gr.
Currently, there have been no changes to requirements for land border crossings between Bulgaria and Greece. The Ministry of Foreign Affairs explained that "all passengers entering Greece via the land border, regardless of their nationality, need to fill out an electronic Passenger Locator Form (PLF) and generate a QR code at least 24 hours before arrival."
Closing Ilinden, Kapitan Petko Voyvoda and Makaza checkpoints led to an increase in traffic flow at the border near Petrich. There were no issues with transit, nor did there exist any truck queues. These people were waiting to sail from Greece. Passengers had to wait about one hour and a half for their transit.
Three checkpoints are open at the border between Bulgaria and Greece. Currently, the section between "Ilinden - Exohi" and Ivaylovgrad is closed.
Dominic Cummings visits the SAS base and is taken hostage.
The Prime Minister’s Chief Adviser experienced the feeling of being held hostage during a visit to the base last week.
For some harassed civil servants, the idea of holding Dominic Cummings hostage by pointing a gun at his head might have an appealing twist.
The hardline Prime Minister’s Chief Advisor experienced first-hand the taste of it when he visited SAS headquarters at Sterling Line last week.
The Sunday Mail reported that part of his itinerary on Wednesday included visiting an "indoors counter-terrorism training room," where elite soldiers hone their skills in dealing with terrorists.
He also toured the Hereford base under guidance, inspected the military equipment of the brigade, heard a briefing on recent operations, including efforts to clear Islamic State terrorists from Iraq.
Important guests to the Sterling line have been accorded special welcome – get an up-close glimpse of “The Indoor Anti-terror Training Room”, where SAS trains close-quarters combat in its two-storey building.
Princess Diana had also visited here in 1983 during a training exercise when her hair was singed by the sparks from an accidentally thrown hand grenade.
It is known that Mr. Cummings arrived by helicopter, and the base is named after Colonel David Stirling, founder of the regiment.
He also visited the nearby Pontrilas military training area, where several other secretive units were stationed.
This visit is one stop on his tour of highly classified British security facilities, which has garnered attention from politicians and military leaders.
When news of its plans was leaked earlier in the month, Conservative backbencher and chairman of the House of Commons Defence Select Committee, Tobias Ellwood, said that MPs should have the same opportunities as ministers and civil servants to ask him about defence and security matters.
The situation of requiring a special advisor appointed by political figures to provide testimony is rare.
According to reports, SAS senior officials used the activities on Wednesday to highlight the importance of maintaining the numbers of the Royal Marines and the Parachute Regiment.
According to earlier reports, units that have been sending large numbers of personnel to special forces could be downsized or merged to save costs.
A defense source said: "SAS consists of 250 soldiers, half of whom were previously paratroopers. The size of the Special Boat Squadron (SBS) is smaller; without the support of the Royal Marines, it may be difficult to sustain."
"Cummings was informed that these units must be retained intact."
Mr Cummings, who has complained that the military procurement pipeline continues to float billions of pounds, has recently attended meetings at MI5 and MI6 headquarters, and plans to visit the SBS headquarters in Poole, Dorsetshire, as well as the national top-secret laboratory in Boulton Down, Wiltshire.
The Ministry of National Defense did not respond to the request for comment last night.
"Sunday Morning": Day Show: A Tribute to Rodgers and Hart
2020 was the centenary of Richard Rodgers and Lorenz Hart's iconic partnership, one of Broadway's most legendary musical theater duos.
In tribute to these two songwriters, Broadway star Jelani Alladin (from Frozen and Hercules), Christiane Pitts (from Kong and Moana), and dancer Richard Riaz Yoder (from Love, Loss and What I Wear and Shuffle Along) performed a modern adaptation of the show – "The Runaway Girl," which was specially recorded for the audience of Sunday Morning. This adaptation is based on Rodgers and Hart’s original work.
Musical director and arranger for the performances is drummer Bryan Carter, with bassist Russell Hall on stage as well.
California fire in Larson County, California, is brought under control.
Two wildfires have broken out in Lassen County, located northeast of Sacramento County in California, with an area burned exceeding 12,141 hectares. Firefighters are still battling to contain the fires.
The California fire department said that as of Saturday morning, the "Gold" wildfire that broke out in the southern part of Agua Dulce had only been brought under control to 40%, with large numbers of houses and ranches still at risk from the blaze, which could have a significant impact on local agriculture, livestock farming, and forestry industries.
The fire has been burning for four days, having destroyed approximately 8,498 hectares of land and seven houses, and injuring two firefighters who were taken to hospital.
Firefighters worked overnight to break through and reinforce the fireline under limited conditions.
But according to the weather forecast, it was expected to be windy and low humidity on that day.
The "Hog" wildfire that erupted in western Susanville has destroyed two buildings and approximately 4,047 hectares of land.
The California Department of Forestry and Fire Protection stated that the fire had been brought under control after firefighters worked through the night to clear and reinforce the fire line.
As of Saturday morning, the wildfire had been burning for six days, with 45% of it brought under control.
Be careful of those seemingly good horticulture tricks.
To plant physi-cians, this might be an uncomfortable matter, but I am indeed very obsessed with horticultural tricks.
On the positive side, I tend to think of myself as an open-minded, or at least somewhat irrational optimist, and many things in the plant world are not easily discernible through intuition.
But I am not ashamed to admit that I have fallen in this gardening trick countless times.
While I firmly believe in the value of trying new things, if I am not keen to open the “hell mode”, I can avoid some of the most common gardening tricks I have encountered below.
Firstly, perhaps the biggest minefield – grafting tomatoes onto potatoes.
Transplanting tomatoes onto potatoes was hailed as a revolutionary breakthrough, though in fact it is an old trick that was attempted back in the 1920s.
Tomatoes and potatoes belong to the same family; their internal “pipes” are roughly similar, so it is indeed possible to produce an enhanced miracle through grafting.
But the problem is that creating resource-intensive structures such as fruit and root crops requires a lot of energy for metabolism, and grafted plants need to meet the energy demands of two crops simultaneously, leaving limited energy to be diverted.
This means that the yield of these grafted plants is very low, quite different from what you see in the promotional pictures.
Moreover, the time of crop maturity varies, so harvesting potatoes requires killing tomatoes that have not yet matured.
At the same time, propaganda images contain many deceptive tricks.
I have seen roses that had been processed into sky blue using PS in some directories, and I've also noticed the number of fruits on certain trees as incredible.
Some merchants even “borrow” photos from my Instagram account and falsely claim them to be their test bases on online shopping websites.
These unscrupulous merchants' actions make you have to look at these promotional images with a skeptical eye, especially those from the internet.
If it looks so good that it doesn’t look real, it probably is.
When it comes to blue rose flowers, blue is relatively rare in the plant world, so if you see new varieties of colorful broom rashan, orchids, or cacti glowing with a blue light under high-concentration 'disinfectants,' it's essentially because they were sprayed with pesticide or dyed.
If suppliers explicitly state the situation, there’s not much to worry about, but they rarely do so.
The blue butterfly orchid will revert to its original white, but cacti and holly ivy will quickly wilt after being coated with an anti-reflective dye.
Finally, I must expose the bonsai set that promises to grow miniature trees, and there are worse bonsai seeds than such sets.
Bonsai is not a plant.
This is a pruning technique reminiscent of ancient trees on distant mountains.
Bonsai are typically mature shrubs that have been pruned and then guided to form new growth directions with wires.
I am aware that I will continue to be captivated by these horticultural tricks, which is one of the pleasures of experimentation.
But by avoiding these issues, you may have an opportunity to purchase an item that matches what the seller is showcasing.
Spanish isolation measures: Do you need to be quarantined in Spain?
No need, but if you return from Spain after midnight tonight, you must quarantine yourself at home for 14 days.
UK Justice Secretary Humza Yousaf said: "We decided earlier this week not to enforce quarantine measures for those returning from Spain because data shows an improvement in the spread of the virus."
But clearly, the latest data warrants our concern, and we therefore have to overturn the original decision.
We know that doing so will disappoint people, but we have always made it clear to everyone that we will closely monitor the epidemic situation in each country and if the virus were to reignite in one nation, we might ask them to be removed from our list of countries not implementing isolation measures altogether.
“Virus remains rampant and deadly. Our top priority is to suppress the virus, prevent its spread, and protect public health.”
The government has not yet announced regulations for British citizens traveling to Spain, but if you plan to travel to Spain, it is best to check the regulations for your area.
Spanish Deputy Director of Emergency Health, Maria Jose Sierra, stated:
"There has been a major outbreak here. This may be the second wave. We need to keep an eye on the situation over the next few weeks."
Imam-Ul-Haq of the Pakistan team continues to compete despite injuring himself during warm-up.
The Pakistan team and the England team will face each other in three Test matches and three T20Is.
Pakistan cricket player Imam-ul-Haq was injured when hit in the left hand during a team warm-up match in a derby, and the team doctor announced that he is now fit to play.
The Pakistan Cricket Board (PCB) stated in a statement that "the cricket player, who is known to use his left hand, participated in a warm-up match before the start of the next day's game."
On the first day of the tournament, when PCB White Team's Imam and PCB Green Team's Naseem Shah exchanged punches, Imam was hit in the left hand.
On Friday night, Imam was injured when his left hand was hit by a ball thrown by Shah and had to withdraw from the game, but he participated in the morning warm-up match and will return to the field on the third day.
More than 30 employees of a bank in Tamil Nadu have tested positive for COVID-19.
Officials from the government departments and a state-owned bank in Tiruguraberi, Tamil Nadu, India, stated that at least 38 employees of a major branch of the bank located in Tiruguraberi tested positive for coronavirus.
Officials say they have suggested that customers who have visited the branch voluntarily participate in virus testing.
Previously, a senior employee of the branch had other complications and later died from COVID-19.
Officials say that recently, they conducted a large-scale medical screening at the branch, which led to the discovery of these positive cases.
The branch manager said that they have completed disinfection and the bank may resume operations tomorrow.
Company health officials are contacting customers who visited that bank.
Tamil Nadu is the second-most affected state in India in terms of confirmed cases, with Maharashtra reporting the highest number of infections. As of Saturday, Tamil Nadu had reported a total of 206,737 confirmed cases.
India's Prime Minister will attend the groundbreaking ceremony for a temple built on a disputed site.
New Delhi (AP) – A trustee overseeing temple construction said Indian Prime Minister Narendra Modi plans to attend next month the groundbreaking ceremony for a Hindu temple being built on a disputed site in northern India, where a mosque that dated back to the 16th century was destroyed by Hindu extremists in 1992.
The ceremony is scheduled to take place on August 5 and organizers say that this day is auspicious for Hindus but also marks one year since the Indian Parliament revoked the semi-autonomous status of Jammu and Kashmir, which has a majority Muslim population.
Modi’s supporters and opponents in India’s Hindu nationalist Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP) will not miss this symbolic day, when the party has long promised to take back Kashmir’s autonomy and build a temple dedicated to the Hindu deity Ram on the site of a mosque that predates the Mughal era, with believers claiming it is the birthplace of Ram. The site is located in Ayodhya, Uttar Pradesh.
The coronavirus continues to spread across India, where it has reportedly become the country with the third-highest number of infections globally. In light of this, the Nationalist Hindu group Vishwa Hindu Parishad, which has an alliance with the ruling Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP), has announced that the ceremony will be live-streamed on national television and that attendance at the event will be limited in both participants and audience members.
The organization's spokesperson, Vinod Bansal, said at a press conference on Saturday that the temple will become "a perpetual center for social harmony, national unity and integration, awakening of Hinduism."
Following the overwhelming victory of the Indian People ' s Party in the elections, a century-long dispute over the site was resolved last year.
In November, the Supreme Court ruled in favor of the temple trust, ordering that five acres be allocated elsewhere for Muslim petitioners.
Hindu extremists have long claimed that Mughal Muslim invaders built a mosque on top of the existing temples in the ancient city of Ayodhya.
The riots that followed the destruction of the mosque in December 1992 were sparked by community violence and resulted in the deaths of approximately 2,000 people, most of whom were Muslims.
Meanwhile, the special court is still hearing the case of the mosque demolition.
An architect from Ahmedabad, Gujarat, India, hometown of Modi, proposes building a tower-shaped sandstone structure temple that is 161 feet (49 meters) high with five domes.
